### Why is SimpleDateFormat not thread-safe?

SimpleDateFormat holds a mutable `Calendar` field internally. When `format(Date)` is called, it writes to this Calendar with `calendar.setTime(date)` and then reads from it with `subFormat()`. When two threads call `format()` on the same instance simultaneously, one thread's write overwrites the other's data, producing garbled output.

```java
// Internal implementation (simplified)
public String format(Date date) {
    calendar.setTime(date);   // Thread-1 writes here
    // Thread-2 overwrites calendar.setTime() at the same moment!
    return subFormat(...);    // reads corrupted Calendar state
}
```

### How do you reproduce the bug?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;
import java.util.Date;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DateFormatBug {
    // WRONG: shared mutable instance
    static final SimpleDateFormat sdf = new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd");

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
        Date date = new Date();

        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
            pool.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    String result = sdf.format(date);
                    System.out.println(result);
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    System.out.println("ERROR: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
                }
            });
        }
        pool.shutdown();
        pool.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

```text
2024-10-25
2024-10-25
ERROR: NumberFormatException
0025-10-25
2024-10-25
```

### What are the three fixes?

#### Fix 1: new instance per call

```java
String formatted = new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd").format(date);
// Safe but creates a new object every call
// At 10,000 calls/second: significant GC pressure
```

#### Fix 2: ThreadLocal

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;
import java.util.Date;

public class DateFormatFixes {

    // Fix 2: ThreadLocal, one instance per thread
    private static final ThreadLocal<SimpleDateFormat> dateFormatter =
        ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd"));

    public static String formatWithThreadLocal(Date date) {
        return dateFormatter.get().format(date);
        // Each thread has its own SimpleDateFormat
        // Remember: dateFormatter.remove() in finally in thread pools!
    }
}
```

#### Fix 3: DateTimeFormatter (best)

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

public class DateFormatBest {

    // Fix 3: immutable, thread-safe, one instance for entire application
    private static final DateTimeFormatter FORMATTER =
        DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd");

    public static String formatDate(LocalDate date) {
        return date.format(FORMATTER);
        // No ThreadLocal needed! No remove() needed!
        // Shared safely by all threads.
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(formatDate(LocalDate.of(2024, 10, 25)));
    }
}
```

```text
2024-10-25
```

### How does DateTimeFormatter compare to SimpleDateFormat?

| Feature | SimpleDateFormat | DateTimeFormatter |
|---|---|---|
| Thread-safe | No | Yes (immutable) |
| Mutable state | Calendar field | None |
| Sharing | Requires ThreadLocal | Share freely |
| API | Date, Calendar | LocalDate, LocalDateTime |
| Java version | 1.1+ | 8+ |
| Performance | Fast per-call | Fast per-call |
| Memory in pools | ThreadLocal overhead | Single static instance |

### What are the java.time equivalents?

```java
// Old API                              New API (java.time)
SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd")    →  DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd")
sdf.format(new Date())            →  LocalDate.now().format(formatter)
sdf.parse("2024-10-18")           →  LocalDate.parse("2024-10-18", formatter)
Date                               →  LocalDate / LocalDateTime / ZonedDateTime
Calendar                           →  LocalDate / LocalDateTime (immutable)
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: static shared SimpleDateFormat
static final SimpleDateFormat sdf = new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd");
// Multiple threads corrupt each other's Calendar

// WRONG: ThreadLocal without remove() in pool
static final ThreadLocal<SimpleDateFormat> tl =
    ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd"));
// Memory leak in thread pool: remove() never called

// CORRECT: DateTimeFormatter, no cleanup needed
static final DateTimeFormatter FMT = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd");
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Why is DateTimeFormatter thread-safe? It is immutable: no internal mutable state to corrupt.
- Is FastDateFormat from Apache Commons thread-safe? Yes, it was created specifically to be a thread-safe alternative to SimpleDateFormat.
- Can you synchronize on SimpleDateFormat instead? Yes, but it serializes all formatting calls and becomes a bottleneck under load.
